title: Block Diagram
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki
tags: MermaidExample

A block diagram arranges nodes on an explicit grid with arrows between them. Unlike flowcharts (which auto-layout), block diagrams give you direct control over column placement. Use it for component architecture, data pipelines, and structured system overviews. ''New in Mermaid 11.''

---

!! Basic example

```
block-beta
    columns 3
    Browser["Browser"] API["API Server"] DB[("Database")]
    Browser --> API --> DB
```

$$$text/vnd.tiddlywiki.mermaid
block-beta
    columns 3
    Browser["Browser"] API["API Server"] DB[("Database")]
    Browser --> API --> DB
$$$

---

!! Real-world example: Web application stack

This diagram maps a web application into four horizontal layers — client, gateway, services, and data — with color coding to distinguish each layer's role.

```
block-beta
    %% ── Client layer ───────────────────────────
    columns 3
    Browser["Browser\nReact SPA"] space CDN["CDN\nCloudFront"]

    %% ── Gateway layer ──────────────────────────
    space LB["Load Balancer\nnginx"] space

    %% ── Service layer ───────────────────────────
    API1["API Server 1\nNode.js"] API2["API Server 2\nNode.js"] Cache[("Redis\nCache")]

    %% ── Data layer ──────────────────────────────
    space DB[("PostgreSQL\nRDS")] space

    %% ── Connections ─────────────────────────────
    Browser --> LB
    CDN --> LB
    LB --> API1
    LB --> API2
    API1 --> Cache
    API2 --> Cache
    API1 --> DB
    API2 --> DB

    %% ── Layer color coding ──────────────────────
    style Browser fill:#6c757d,stroke:#495057,color:#fff
    style CDN fill:#6c757d,stroke:#495057,color:#fff
    style LB fill:#0d6efd,stroke:#084298,color:#fff
    style API1 fill:#198754,stroke:#0f5132,color:#fff
    style API2 fill:#198754,stroke:#0f5132,color:#fff
    style Cache fill:#fd7e14,stroke:#984c0c,color:#fff
    style DB fill:#fd7e14,stroke:#984c0c,color:#fff
```

